    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, using the pattern to mask the wear. I'll tell the client about the granite vs marble thing. One last thing—in a high-traffic environment like this, do you see much issue with 'unexpected' damage? Like, besides just normal foot traffic, are there specific types of damage that tend to pop up out of nowhere that we should be prepared for from a maintenance standpoint?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Unexpected damage in a high-traffic lobby often comes from things *other* than just footsteps, which is where the maintenance planning gets tricky. Think about the things that spill, scrape, or impact things suddenly.\n\nFor stone, the biggest \"unexpected\" threat is chemical damage—acidic spills from coffee, cleaning agents, or even certain types of hand sanitizer that can etch the surface over time. With concrete, the risk is more about impact damage or chemical staining from liquids seeping into the pores. LVT, being synthetic, is usually the most resistant to these sudden chemical shocks, though heavy, sharp impacts can still cause chips.\n\nIt's always wise to have a plan for *spill response*, not just routine cleaning. A quick wipe of a spilled espresso before it sets can prevent a permanent mark on any of these surfaces.",
      "source": "target",
